NAME
       sed - stream editor

SYNOPSIS
       sed [-nEsuz] [-i[SUFFIX]] [-l N] [-e script]... [-f
          file]... [script] [file...]

DESCRIPTION
       The POSIX stream editor with the GNU extensions that
       scripts use: -i, -E, -s, -z, addresses 0,/re/
       first~step addr,+N addr,~N, the I and M flags, commands
       F Q R T W z v, \n \t \xHH and friends in scripts, and
       \L \U \l \u \E in replacements. Regular expressions
       come from the C library (regcomp). Not implemented: the
       e command and the e flag of s (they run a shell).  -i
       writes each file to a temporary file in the same
       directory and renames it over the original. A SUFFIX
       keeps the original under a new name: without a '*' the
       suffix is appended; with '*', each '*' is replaced by
       the file's name, and a result without '/' is placed in
       the file's directory.

NOTES
       sed -i writes a temporary file in the file's directory
       and renames it over the original. A long script yields
       to the kernel every 65,536 commands so the system stays
       responsive.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1)

